\DOC PACKED_DIVSTEP_TAC

\TYPE {PACKED_DIVSTEP_TAC : int -> int list -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal about an {eventually} property by one block
of machine steps, replacing the state-describing assumptions for the current
state with the corresponding assumptions for a new, later state.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments are {n : int}, {shf : int list} and {s : int}. In the observed
traces {n} is an unbound index appearing in the expression supplied for {s};
{shf} is a one-element list of integers; {s} is an arithmetic expression in {n}
giving the step/state numbering used for the new state. Together they determine
which block of execution is taken, and hence the new state name and
program-counter offset. The conclusion of the goal has the form
{eventually ... P s_old}, the {eventually} property applied to a current state
variable; the tactic produces exactly one subgoal whose conclusion is the same
property applied to a fresh later state variable {s_new}. All assumptions
mentioning the old state — the {bytes_loaded} assumption, every
{read <component> s_old = ...} equation, the flag assumptions, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE ... s0 s_old} assumption — are discharged and replaced
by analogous assumptions about {s_new}, with the register/flag right-hand sides
advanced (for example the step index inside the symbolic {divstep_*} terms
incremented and the program-counter offset increased); the {MAYCHANGE}
assumption may also gain further components. Assumptions not mentioning the
state (the arithmetic side conditions, the nonoverlapping hypothesis, and the
definitional equations) are kept unchanged, although the order in which
assumptions are listed can change.
{
   A_nostate, A_state(s_old) ?- eventually ... P s_old
   =============================================  PACKED_DIVSTEP_TAC n shf s
   A_nostate, A_state'(s_new) ?- eventually ... P s_new
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of outcome, always on goals
whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement about a state with a large set of
such state-describing assumptions.

\ENDDOC
